---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = "Shanghai Travel Guide: The Most Foreigner-Friendly City";
const description = "Shanghai guide — attractions, food, transport, and where to stay in China's most international city.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en">
    <nav class="mb-8 not-prose">
      <a href="/en/cities/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 transition no-underline group">
        <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5 transition-transform group-hover:-translate-x-0.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"/>
        </svg>
        Back to City Guides
      </a>
    </nav>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-3">🏙️ Shanghai</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">Shanghai Travel Guide: The Most Foreigner-Friendly City</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-6">Shanghai guide — attractions, food, transport, and where to stay in China's most international city.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <span>📅 Last updated: June 2026</span>
        <span>⏱️ 10 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-yellow-50 to-amber-50 border-l-4 border-yellow-500 rounded-r-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-yellow-900 mb-4">⚡ Quick Answer</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-yellow-800 leading-relaxed">Shanghai is <strong>China's most foreigner-friendly city</strong>. It has the largest English-speaking population, the most international restaurants, and a skyline that rivals any in the world. From the iconic Bund waterfront and the soaring Shanghai Tower to the historic charm of Yu Garden and the magic of Shanghai Disneyland — there is something for everyone. If you're nervous about the language barrier or culture shock, Shanghai is the gentlest introduction to China.</p>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">📋 In This Guide</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        <li><a href="#why-visit" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🌆 Why Visit Shanghai?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-many-days" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">📅 How Many Days Do You Need?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#attractions" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🎯 Top Attractions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#getting-around" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🚇 Getting Around</a></li>
        <li><a href="#where-to-stay" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🏨 Where to Stay</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-to-eat" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🍜 What to Eat</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="why-visit" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🌆 Why Visit Shanghai?</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-12">Shanghai feels like a city of the future. The Pudong skyline — with the Oriental Pearl Tower and Shanghai Tower piercing the clouds — is the face of modern China. But the old city still has charm: the tree-lined streets of the former French Concession, the art deco buildings along the Bund, and the winding lanes of Old Town. Add to that one of the most unique Disney parks in the world and a food scene that ranges from Michelin-starred fine dining to dollar-a-plate street stalls, and you have a city that truly has it all.</p>

    <h2 id="how-many-days" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">📅 How Many Days Do You Need?</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-lg text-gray-700 mb-12">
      <li><strong>3 days (standard):</strong> The Bund, Old Town, former French Concession, and a skyline visit to Shanghai Tower or Oriental Pearl</li>
      <li><strong>4–5 days (comfortable):</strong> Add Shanghai Disneyland, Zhujiajiao Water Town, or a day trip to nearby Suzhou</li>
      <li><strong>5+ days (deep dive):</strong> Explore the city's museums, art galleries, and nightlife neighborhoods in depth</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- ==================== ATTRACTIONS ==================== -->
    <h2 id="attractions" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🎯 Top Attractions</h2>

    <!-- The Bund (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/the-bund.webp" alt="The Bund waterfront at night with colonial buildings and Pudong skyline" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">The Bund (外滩)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">Shanghai's iconic waterfront promenade is a must-see for every visitor. On one side, a row of 26 magnificent colonial-era buildings — the "Exposition of World Architecture" — showcasing Gothic, Baroque, Romanesque, and Art Deco styles. On the other, the dazzling Pudong skyline with the Oriental Pearl Tower and Shanghai Tower lighting up the night. The Bund is best visited at sunset, when the golden light hits the old buildings and the city lights gradually flicker on across the river. A leisurely walk along the 1.5-kilometer promenade is the perfect way to start your Shanghai experience.</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Shanghai Tower (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/shanghai-tower.webp" alt="Shanghai Tower, China's tallest skyscraper" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Shanghai Tower (上海中心大厦)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">China's tallest building and the third tallest in the world at 632 meters. The observation deck at 546 meters offers a jaw-dropping 360-degree view over all of Shanghai — on a clear day you can see the Huangpu River snaking all the way to the sea. The elevator ride is an experience in itself: it reaches a speed of 20 meters per second (72 km/h) and you'll feel your ears pop as you rocket up. Inside, the tower also features the world's highest hotel (J-Hotel) and a stunning atrium that spirals all the way up the building. Book tickets online in advance to avoid long queues.</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Oriental Pearl Tower (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/oriental-pearl.webp" alt="Oriental Pearl Tower, iconic symbol of Shanghai" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Oriental Pearl Tower (东方明珠塔)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">No skyline is more recognizable than the Oriental Pearl Tower. Though it is no longer Shanghai's tallest structure, the Oriental Pearl remains the city's most iconic symbol, appearing on everything from postcards to T-shirts. The tower features 11 spheres of varying sizes connected by three columns. The "Space Module" at 351 meters offers a glass-floor observation deck where you can look straight down at the city below. At the base is the Shanghai History Museum and a shopping complex. For the best value, buy a combo ticket that includes the observation deck, the glass-bottom walk, and the history museum. Visit at sunset for the most dramatic views.</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Yu Garden (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/yu-garden.webp" alt="Yu Garden's traditional pavilions and pond" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Yu Garden (豫园)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">A 400-year-old Ming Dynasty garden hidden in the heart of Shanghai's Old Town. Built in 1559 by a government official as a private garden for his parents, Yu Garden is a masterpiece of classical Chinese landscape design — complete with pavilions, rockeries, ponds, and zigzag bridges that are meant to confuse evil spirits (who can only travel in straight lines). The Exquisite Jade Rock (玉玲珑) is the garden's centerpiece: a 3.3-meter porous stone that is over 1,000 years old. Surrounding the garden is the Yuyuan Bazaar, a lively market area packed with snack stalls, souvenir shops, and the famous Nanxiang Steamed Bun Restaurant where xiaolongbao (soup dumplings) were invented. Allow 2–3 hours to explore the garden and the bazaar.</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Wukang Road (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/wukang-road.webp" alt="Wukang Mansion and tree-lined streets in the Former French Concession area" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Wukang Road & Anfu Road (武康路/安福路) — Former French Concession Area</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">Once the heart of the French Concession (法租界, 1849–1943), this area is now known as the <strong>Wukang Road–Anfu Road Historical & Cultural District</strong>. This is the trendiest part of Shanghai: tree-lined streets, art deco apartment buildings from the 1920s, and hundreds of independent boutiques, cafés, and restaurants. The iconic Wukang Mansion (武康大楼), a striking ship-like building at the junction of Wukang and Huaihai Roads, is the most photographed spot in the district. Wukang Road is quieter and more residential — perfect for a relaxed stroll. Anfu Road is livelier, packed with craft coffee shops, designer stores, and some of the best brunch spots in the city. Spend a morning or afternoon café-hopping and people-watching; this is Shanghai at its most cosmopolitan.</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Jing'an Temple (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/jingan-temple-v2.webp" alt="Jing'an Temple golden rooftop complex surrounded by modern skyscrapers" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Jing'an Temple (静安寺)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">One of China's oldest Buddhist temples, with roots stretching back to 247 AD. The current buildings are a 2010 reconstruction in traditional Song dynasty style, but the temple's name and site have endured for over 1,700 years. The most striking sight is the main hall's golden roof — it gleams in the afternoon sun and looks almost surreal against the glass towers of Jing'an District. Inside, a 3.8-meter jade Buddha sits in the center hall, carved from a single block of Burmese jade. The temple is surrounded by one of Shanghai's busiest shopping districts, so the contrast between the incense-filled courtyard and the luxury malls next door is jarring in the best way. Entry is 50 RMB. Go on a weekday morning for the quietest experience.</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Nanjing Road (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/nanjing-road-v2.webp" alt="Nanjing Road pedestrian street at night with neon lights and crowds" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Nanjing Road (南京路)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">China's most famous shopping street, and the stretch that connects the Bund to People's Square. The pedestrian section — about 1.2 kilometers of walking-only pavement — is a spectacle of neon, department stores, and street performers that feels closer to Times Square than anything else in China. The historic wing (East Nanjing Road) still has the 1930s art deco façades of the old Shanghai department stores; the modern wing (West Nanjing Road) is where you'll find the luxury flagship stores. You don't need to buy anything. The point is to walk it at night, when the signs flicker on and the crowd flows in both directions like a living river. If you do want to shop, the First Foodhall at the East end has been selling Shanghai snacks since 1934.</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Zhujiajiao Water Town (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/zhujiajiao.webp" alt="Canals and stone bridges of Zhujiajiao Water Town" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Zhujiajiao Water Town (朱家角)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">An ancient water town just 45 minutes by subway from downtown Shanghai. Zhujiajiao (literally "Zhu Family Village") has a history spanning over 1,700 years, with well-preserved Ming and Qing Dynasty architecture. The town is crisscrossed by canals lined with willow trees, crossed by 36 stone bridges — the most famous being Fangsheng Bridge (放生桥), a 5-arch stone bridge built in 1571. Take a traditional wooden gondola (similar to Venice's gondolas but far more affordable) through the canals, visit the Kezhi Garden (课植园), and sample local snacks like zongzi (sticky rice dumplings) and stinky tofu. Zhujiajiao is less commercialized than the more famous Zhouzhuang, making it a more authentic and relaxed experience. A half-day trip is enough, but you can also stay overnight in a canal-side guesthouse for a truly peaceful experience.</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Shanghai Disneyland (long text → float wrap) -->
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="float-left mr-8 mb-4 w-full md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/shanghai/disneyland.webp" alt="Enchanted Storybook Castle at Shanghai Disneyland" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Shanghai Disneyland (上海迪士尼乐园)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">Shanghai Disneyland is the most unique Disney resort in the world. Opened in 2016, it features the <strong>Enchanted Storybook Castle</strong> — the largest Disney castle ever built at 60 meters tall. Its signature attraction, <strong>TRON Lightcycle Power Run</strong>, is widely considered one of the best thrill rides at any Disney park. The park also offers exclusive attractions like <strong>Pirates of the Caribbean: Battle for the Sunken Treasure</strong> and authentic Chinese cuisine that you won't find at any other Disney resort. The park is about 40 minutes from downtown Shanghai by subway (Line 11, direct to the gate).</p>
      <div class="clear-both"></div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="getting-around" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🚇 Getting Around</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg text-gray-700 mb-12">
      <li><strong>Subway:</strong> Shanghai has the longest metro system in the world (19 lines, 800+ km). Everything is labeled in English. Check the <a href="/en/transport/metro-bus/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Metro & Bus Guide for Major Chinese Cities</a> for route tips.</li>
      <li><strong>Didi:</strong> Works perfectly. Use it for short trips or when the subway doesn't reach. Get setup tips in <a href="/en/transport/didi/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">How to Use Didi in China as a Foreigner</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Maglev (磁悬浮):</strong> The world's fastest commercial train, connecting Pudong Airport to downtown (7 minutes, 430 km/h). Worth doing for the experience alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Biking:</strong> Shanghai is flat and bike-friendly. Shared bikes (Hellobike, Meituan) are everywhere in the city center — perfect for exploring the Wukang Road and Anfu Road area.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="where-to-stay" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🏨 Where to Stay</h2>
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6"><h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">The Bund / Lujiazui (外滩/陆家嘴) — Best for first-time visitors</h3><p class="text-gray-700">Iconic skyline views, close to major attractions, plenty of luxury hotels. Pricier but worth the splurge for the experience.</p></div>
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6"><h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Wukang Road Area / 原法租界 (武康路) — Best for foodies and culture lovers</h3><p class="text-gray-700">The trendiest area with great restaurants, bars, and boutiques. Quiet streets shaded by plane trees, perfect for morning walks. This was once the heart of the French Concession (1849–1943).</p></div>
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12"><h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Jing'an (静安) — Best for shopping and nightlife</h3><p class="text-gray-700">A vibrant commercial district with excellent metro connections. Close to Nanjing Road and Jing'an Temple.</p></div>

    <!-- ==================== WHAT TO EAT ==================== -->
    <h2 id="what-to-eat" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🍜 What to Eat</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Shanghai's food scene is legendary. Here are the dishes you absolutely must try.</p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-12">
      <!-- Xiaolongbao -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/shanghai/food/xiaolongbao.webp" alt="Xiaolongbao soup dumplings steamed pork buns" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Xiaolongbao (小笼包)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Delicate dumplings wrapped in paper-thin, pleated dough, filled with minced pork and a jellied pork stock that melts into a silky, intensely savory broth when steamed. To eat: nibble a small hole in the side, blow to cool the scalding liquid inside, then slurp the soup before dipping the whole dumpling in black vinegar with fine ginger shreds. The best ones have at least 18 pleats and a thin, translucent wrapper that doesn't tear. You'll find them in most dim sum houses around the Old City and especially near Yu Garden — follow the steam.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Shengjianbao -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/shanghai/food/shengjianbao.webp" alt="Shengjianbao pan-fried pork buns crispy bottom" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Shengjianbao (生煎包)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Yeasted buns fried bottom-up in a wide iron pan until the base turns into a thick, crunchy golden crust while the top stays soft and pillowy. The pork filling inside is seasoned with ginger and soy, releasing a hot, peppery soup with every bite they won't let you forget. Eat them fresh off the pan — they're usually sold in batches of four in a paper box, and they stay volcanic for minutes. Look for long queues outside small storefronts in the Old City and along Food Streets; that queue is your guarantee.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Hong Shao Rou -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/shanghai/food/hongshaorou.webp" alt="Hong Shao Rou red braised pork belly soy sauce" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Red Braised Pork — Hong Shao Rou (红烧肉)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Thick slabs of pork belly with alternating ribbons of lean meat and translucent fat, braised low and slow in dark soy sauce, rock sugar, and Shaoxing wine until the fat turns gelatinous and the sauce reduces to a glossy, almost sticky glaze. Each cube is simultaneously tender and unctuous, with a complex sweetness that balances the deep savory punch of the soy. Served alongside braised eggs and bamboo shoots, it demands a bowl of plain steamed rice to soak up every last drop of the dark caramel sauce. Found on the menu at nearly every Shanghainese home-style restaurant, from the Old City to the French Concession.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Scallion Oil Noodles -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/shanghai/food/congyoubanmian.webp" alt="Cong You Ban Mian scallion oil noodles simple classic" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Scallion Oil Noodles — Cong You Ban Mian (葱油拌面)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Deceptively simple: springy wheat noodles tossed in oil that has been slowly infused with charred scallions until fragrant and nutty, then seasoned with soy sauce and a pinch of sugar. Each strand comes out slick and glossy, carrying a subtle smokiness from the scallions and just enough salt to keep you coming back for another bite. Some stalls add dried shrimp or crispy fried shallots on top for extra texture. A fast, cheap lunch staple found at food courts, street-side noodle shops, and hole-in-the-wall canteens all over Shanghai.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Hairy Crab -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/shanghai/food/dazhaxie.webp" alt="Da Zha Xie hairy crab Yangcheng Lake autumn delicacy" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Crab — Da Zha Xie (大闸蟹)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">A seasonal obsession that takes over Shanghai every autumn — these freshwater crabs from nearby Yangcheng Lake are prized for their golden, buttery roe (in females) and sweet, delicate meat (in males). The name comes from the thick hair covering their claws, which signals maturity. Cracking one open with the traditional wooden mallet is a ritual: you pick out every sliver of meat, dip it in black vinegar with julienned ginger, and wash it down with warm Shaoxing wine. During crab season (September to December), you'll see them advertised everywhere from high-end restaurants to street stalls across the city.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="not-prose mt-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8">📚 Related Guides</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <a href="/en/cities/city-select/" class="block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-blue-300 transition">🗺️ <strong>How Many Days in China?</strong> — Plan your full itinerary</a>
        <a href="/en/cities/beijing-guide/" class="block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-blue-300 transition">🏛️ <strong>Beijing Travel Guide</strong> — See how Shanghai compares</a>
        <a href="/en/transport/metro-bus/" class="block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-blue-300 transition">🚇 <strong>Metro & Bus Guide for Major Chinese Cities</strong> — Navigate Shanghai's subway</a>
        <a href="/en/payment/payment-guide/" class="block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-blue-300 transition">💳 <strong>How to Pay in China</strong> — Alipay and WeChat Pay setup</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
